## 3. Customer Reality

### 3.1 Jobs to Be Done

The buyer is not hiring AgentOSync to “use AI.” They are trying to:

- respond to every valuable inquiry within an acceptable window;
- know which leads have not received a next action;
- recover missed calls and after-hours demand;
- prevent quotes, proposals, or appointments from going stale;
- remove repetitive data entry and coordination;
- see the state of revenue workflows without chasing staff;
- make automation safe enough to trust;
- prove whether a workflow change created value.

### 3.2 Trigger Events

Content becomes relevant when one of these occurs:

- the owner discovers leads sitting untouched;
- marketing spend increases but booked appointments do not;
- a key employee leaves and takes process knowledge with them;
- a new CRM or phone system is introduced;
- response-time complaints rise;
- the team adds another inbox or acquisition channel;
- the owner is manually checking spreadsheets, messages, and CRM records;
- a competitor begins advertising AI reception, instant response, or 24/7 coverage;
- the company wants AI but cannot identify a safe first workflow.

### 3.3 Current Workarounds

- shared spreadsheets;
- personal reminders;
- manual CRM tasks;
- group chats;
- call-forwarding;
- reception or outsourced answering services;
- generic CRM sequences;
- disconnected Zapier or Make automations;
- founder inspection and staff follow-up meetings.

These workarounds should not be mocked. Each exists because it solved part of the problem. AgentOSync content should explain where it fails at scale and what a controlled upgrade looks like.

### 3.4 Buyer Anxieties and Required Proof

| Anxiety | Proof AgentOSync must provide |
| --- | --- |
| “Will this message customers incorrectly?” | Draft/approval boundary, exception queue, and sample escalation rules |
| “Will this replace staff?” | Workload map showing repetitive tasks removed and decisions retained |
| “Will it integrate?” | Named systems, data flow, system of record, and fallback path |
| “How do we know it worked?” | Baseline, event definitions, weekly metrics, and observation window |
| “Is this another demo?” | Live workflow evidence, logs, approved testimonial, or clearly labelled demo proof |
| “What happens when it fails?” | Human escalation, retry policy, rollback, and owner |
| “Will this become a giant project?” | One-workflow scope, fixed discovery artifact, and explicit out-of-scope list |

