Recommended mix for the first month:

- 35% workflow diagnostics;
- 25% practical education;
- 20% approval and trust architecture;
- 15% proof, teardown, or build-in-public observations;
- 5% direct offer posts.

## 6. LinkedIn Hook System

A hook must create relevance, not merely curiosity. It should identify a recognizable situation, make a specific claim, or expose a costly contradiction.

### 6.1 Hook Families

#### Cost-of-leak

- The most expensive lead is the one you already paid for—and never followed up with.
- Your CRM probably isn’t losing leads. Your handoffs are.
- A missed call at 7:03 p.m. can expose an entire operating problem.

#### Contrarian

- Most businesses do not have an AI problem. They have an ownership problem.
- Before you buy an AI receptionist, map what happens after the call.
- Automating a broken handoff only makes the failure happen faster.

#### Diagnostic

- If a lead arrives right now, can you answer these five questions?
- Seven places a paid lead disappears before anyone notices.
- Use this 10-minute test before automating your follow-up.

#### Story

- A business owner told me, “We respond to every lead.” The CRM showed something else.
- We mapped one missed call from ring to booked appointment. The problem was not the phone.
- The automation looked perfect until the first customer replied with an exception.

#### Proof or teardown

- Here is the exact event-to-approval map for a safe missed-call recovery workflow.
- This is what “human in the loop” should mean in practice.
- Five metrics separate an AI demo from an operating workflow.

### 6.2 Hook Quality Test

Score each hook from 0–2 on:

- ICP recognizability;
- business consequence;
- specificity;
- credibility;
- tension or curiosity;
- fit with the body.

Reject any hook below 8/12. Reject it regardless of score if the post cannot substantiate the promise.

### 6.3 First Three Lines

The opening should do three jobs:

1. name the problem or contradiction;
2. show why it matters;
3. create a reason to continue.

Example:

> The most expensive lead is the one you already paid for—and never followed up with.  
> Most teams call this a sales problem.  
> It is usually a workflow problem hiding between five systems and three people.

## 7. Post Structures

### 7.1 Diagnostic Post

1. Hook.
2. Familiar scenario.
3. Root-cause reframe.
4. Diagnostic list or framework.
5. Small action the reader can take.
6. Relevant question or lead-magnet CTA.

### 7.2 Point-of-View Post

1. Defensible contrarian claim.
2. Explain the common approach.
3. Show where it fails.
4. Offer a more useful model.
5. Give one concrete example.
6. Invite practitioners to challenge or extend it.

### 7.3 Teardown / Proof Post

1. State the workflow and context.
2. Show the starting condition.
3. Map event → decision → action → approval → evidence.
4. Identify exceptions.
5. State what was measured.
6. Label proof status: concept, demo, pilot, or measured production result.

### 7.4 Document / Infographic Post

Use a document when the reader benefits from saving, sharing, or applying a framework.

Recommended slide sequence:

1. Outcome-led title.
2. The expensive misconception.
3. The workflow diagram.
4–8. One leak or diagnostic question per slide.
9. The smallest safe intervention.
10. Checklist and CTA.

Each slide should communicate one idea. The caption must still provide useful context; the visual should not carry unsupported claims.

### 7.5 Video Post—Later Stage

Use Ammar’s avatar only after written posts identify winning problems and language. A video should adapt a validated idea, not become the experiment itself.

Structure:

- 0–3 seconds: three-to-seven-word on-screen problem hook;
- 3–12 seconds: scenario and consequence;
- 12–35 seconds: three-part insight or diagnostic;
- 35–50 seconds: practical next step;
- final seconds: one CTA.

The avatar is a production multiplier, not a substitute for a market-backed point of view.

## 8. Recommended First LinkedIn Post

### 8.1 Post Draft

**The most expensive lead is the one you already paid for—and never followed up with.**

Most teams call this a sales problem.

It is usually a workflow problem hiding between five systems and three people.

A customer calls after hours.  
The call is missed.  
A notification goes to one person.  
The customer also fills out a form.  
Someone creates a CRM record the next morning.  
Nobody knows whether the two inquiries belong to the same person.  
And the owner sees the problem only when the customer has already chosen someone else.

Before buying another AI tool, map these seven points:

1. Where does the inquiry first arrive?
2. Who owns the first response?
3. What is the response-time target?
4. Where is the next action recorded?
5. What happens outside business hours?
6. Which replies require human approval?
7. What evidence proves the loop was completed?

That map tells you whether you need a new tool—or simply a better operating loop.

The safest first AI workflow is rarely “let the agent handle everything.”

It is usually:

> detect the event → prepare the next action → ask for approval when needed → record the outcome → escalate the exception.

That is the difference between an AI demo and an operating system your team can trust.

Where does follow-up break most often in your business: calls, forms, DMs, quotes, or CRM handoffs?

### 8.2 Visual Companion

Title:

> **7 places a paid lead disappears**

Visual flow:

`Inquiry → identity match → owner → first response → next action → approval/exception → outcome recorded`

For each stage, show:

- the failure mode;
- the signal that reveals it;
- the smallest control.

Do not claim a percentage of leads or revenue is lost unless a cited dataset or client baseline supports it.

### 8.3 Why This Should Resonate

- It begins with an economic consequence, not AI enthusiasm.
- It is recognizable across several service-business subsegments.
- It teaches a diagnostic the reader can use immediately.
- It introduces approval-gated AI without jargon.
- It invites operational answers that can reveal ICP language and future post ideas.
- It creates a natural bridge to the AI Workflow Leak Map.

